<template>
    <heading>Outermost Code</heading>
    <keywords>
        <li>doctype</li>
        <li>meta</li>
    </keywords>
    <section data-lp5-kind="explanation">
        <p>
    Note that this code section has no name,
    and recall the lp5 code generation algorithm:
    it first gathers, in order, all the unnamed
    code sections,
    then starts recursively expanding the code
    references they contain.
    But because HTML is a (nearly) rigorously nested syntax,
    that means that when you're using lp5 to write
    an HTML5 program (i.e., a single HTML file),
    there will be one and only one unnamed code section
    (unless you want to give <code>doctype</code>
    its very own lonely little code section).
</p>
        <p>
    The code here contains an HTML <code>&lt;script&gt;</code>
    element and its closing <code>&lt;/script&gt;</code> tag.
    CDATA keeps those tags as literal code in this XML chunk.
</p>
    </section>
    <section data-lp5-kind="code">
        <name/>
        <code><![CDATA[<!doctype html>
<!-- lp5.html <>  -->
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" >
    <title>lp5</title>
    <style>
        ]]><lp5- ref="Global CSS declarations"/><![CDATA[
    </style>
    <script>
        (function () {
            ]]><lp5- ref="Outermost scope code"/><![CDATA[
        )();
    </script>
</head>
<body>
</body>
</html>
]]></code>
    </section>
</template>
